---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import AttentionTrace from "../../components/AttentionTrace.astro";
import DownloadCta from "../../components/DownloadCta.astro";
import { articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from "../../lib/site";

const path = "/how-it-works/";
const title = "How Look Me Detects Blinks and Screen Sessions Locally";
const description = "A transparent explanation of Look Me's on-device MediaPipe pipeline, blink sequence detection, screen and posture intervals, local storage, and failure behavior.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  schemas={[
    articleSchema({ title, description, path }),
    breadcrumbSchema([{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "How it works", path }]),
  ]}
>
  <SiteHeader currentPath={path} />
  <main id="main-content">
    <header class="page-hero shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE LOCAL SIGNAL PIPELINE</p>
      <h1>One camera frame. Three small state machines. No upload.</h1>
      <p>Look Me uses Google MediaPipe Face Landmarker on your computer, then sends the same inferred face state to independent blink, mouth, and posture logic. The renderer stores behavior events—not frames.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="shell"><AttentionTrace /></div>

    <section class="section-tight" aria-labelledby="pipeline-heading">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <div><p class="eyebrow">FRAME TO EVENT</p><h2 id="pipeline-heading">The pipeline is deliberately narrow.</h2></div>
          <p>Each layer has a different responsibility: acquire a frame, infer landmarks locally, decide whether a behavior occurred, then store the smallest useful event.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-rail">
          <article class="feature-row"><span class="feature-time">01 / CAMERA</span><h3>Acquire only while monitoring can run</h3><p>Monitoring respects the master switch, configured schedule, lock and sleep state, camera permission, and page visibility. Pausing releases the camera stream.</p></article>
          <article class="feature-row"><span class="feature-time">02 / MEDIAPIPE</span><h3>Infer face signals on the device</h3><p>MediaPipe processes frames in memory. Look Me reads a small set of blendshape values and landmarks needed for blink, mouth, and posture logic.</p></article>
          <article class="feature-row"><span class="feature-time">03 / SIGNALS</span><h3>Confirm behavior over time</h3><p>A blink is an open–close–open sequence, a yawn requires a sustained mouth opening, and posture uses a learned stable baseline rather than a single coordinate.</p></article>
          <article class="feature-row"><span class="feature-time">04 / EVENTS</span><h3>Store facts, not interpretations</h3><p>The timeline records six event types: blink, yawn, and the start or end of screen and seated intervals. Reminder prompts remain transient runtime state.</p></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-tint" aria-labelledby="blink-heading">
      <div class="shell-narrow article-body">
        <p class="eyebrow">BLINK CONFIRMATION</p>
        <h2 id="blink-heading">Why a blink needs more than one frame</h2>
        <p>A small eyelid coefficient can come from distance, angle, noise, or one-eye movement. Look Me checks the timing of both eyes and uses an eye-aspect-ratio signal to confirm weaker distant-face changes. A long closure is not counted as a series of blinks.</p>
        <p>When sensing confidence drops, the correct result is “unavailable,” not a guessed blink rate. The no-blink reminder pauses until a usable signal returns.</p>

        <h2 id="screen-heading">What “screen time” means inside Look Me</h2>
        <p>Look Me does not inspect screen content. A screen interval means monitoring is active and the app has a usable presence signal. That makes it a practical local activity measure, not a claim about attention, productivity, gaze direction, or the exact distance between your eyes and the display.</p>

        <h2 id="posture-heading">How stand-up events avoid obvious false positives</h2>
        <p>The posture signal first learns a stable seated baseline. A completed rise can end a seated interval and count as standing up. Sudden tracking loss, sideways departure, or an unavailable camera ends the interval differently and is not promoted into a successful stand-up event.</p>

        <div class="article-callout">
          <strong>Observable behavior is not a health score</strong>
          <p>The app intentionally avoids judging whether your blink rate, posture, or screen time is medically healthy. It stores events so you can review your own rhythm.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="storage-heading">What persists for 30 days</h2>
        <p>The timeline is stored in IndexedDB in the renderer profile. It keeps event timestamps and paired interval identifiers, then removes records older than 30 days. Raw frames, face landmark arrays, mouth-opening sequences, and posture calibration coordinates are not saved.</p>

        <p><a class="text-link" href="/privacy/">Read the full privacy inventory<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <DownloadCta />
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
